  data:  # ff2d
    kind: work_item  # ff2d
    title: "ADR-0023 §3 runtime corpus-based test: assert edge_type is constant per (src.*, dst.*) partition"  # ff2d
    status: todo_soft  # ff2d
    priority: 3  # ff2d
    parent:  # ff2d
    tags:  # ff2d
    - concept_audit  # ff2d
    - agent_infrastructure  # ff2d
    - analysis_quality  # ff2d
    - bakeoff_infrastructure  # ff2d
    isbefore: []  # ff2d
    duplicate_of: []  # ff2d
    not_duplicate_of: []  # ff2d
    pr_ref:  # ff2d
    description: "ADR-0023 §3 specifies two complementary enforcement mechanisms for the edge-type axis principle: a static AST-walk lint (built and live) and a runtime corpus-based test (planned but not built). This item covers the runtime half.\n\nCONTEXT (for a dev picking this up cold):\n\nADR-0023 (docs/adr/0023-edge-type-relationship-not-endpoints.md, currently Draft) establishes that edge_type should NAME the relationship between src and dst, not endpoint properties. ADR-0023 §3 names two enforcement mechanisms:\n\nSTATIC (built and live as of PRs #3459 / #3462):\n- find_axis_drift in hypergumbo_core.edge_types — subset-of-registry check on consumer-side *EDGE_TYPE*-named sets.\n- Wired into pre-commit (scripts/check-edge-type-drift) and CI (test_edge_types.py property test).\n- Catches consumer-side drift (sets going stale relative to the registry).\n\nRUNTIME (planned but NOT built):\nADR-0023 §3 specifies the test as:\n\n> test_edge_type_does_not_encode_endpoint_metadata:\n>   for each emitted edge in a corpus run:\n>     assert edge.edge_type is not derivable from\n>       (src.kind, src.language, src.framework,\n>        dst.kind, dst.language, dst.framework)\n>     by any pure function.\n\n> The test is implemented as a coverage check: running the analyzers on a representative corpus, partition emitted edges by (src.kind, src.language, src.framework, dst.kind, dst.language, dst.framework) and assert that within each partition, edge_type is constant up to a short allow-list. Allow-list growth requires a corresponding ADR amendment.\n\nThe two are complementary: static catches consumer-side drift; runtime catches producer-side endpoint-shape leakage (the same partition produces different edge_types depending on framework / language, indicating the type label is encoding endpoint information).\n\nDELIVERABLES:\n\n1. IMPLEMENTATION. Two natural shapes:\n   - Standalone CLI script: scripts/check-edge-type-runtime-coherence reads a behavior-map JSON, computes partitions, reports offending ones. Invocable on any hypergumbo run output.\n   - Bakeoff pipeline integration: hooks into BROAD or DEEP bakeoff cycles so coherence is checked automatically every cycle.\n   Recommendation: build the standalone CLI first (small, testable in isolation, immediately useful during Phase 2/3 migration); integrate with bakeoff as a follow-on.\n\n2. ALLOW-LIST MANAGEMENT. The ADR specifies that allow-list growth requires an ADR amendment. The allow-list is the set of (partition-key → {permitted_edge_types}) entries that are explicitly OK despite having multiple edge_types per partition.\n   - Initial allow-list seeded from a current production run; any partition with multiple edge_types becomes a candidate, surfaced for review during the implementation.\n   - Subsequent additions require an ADR amendment (new ADR or amendment to ADR-0023 / ADR-0024).\n   - Format: a YAML file at docs/edge-type-runtime-allowlist.yaml, or a Python data structure colocated with hypergumbo_core.edge_types.\n\n3. REPORTING AND GATING.\n   - Initial deployment: WARN-only (per ADR-0023 §6 Phase 1 framing — show offenders without blocking). The migration phases shrink the offender set; after Phase 4 it should be empty modulo the allow-list.\n   - Eventual deployment: FAIL on any partition with un-allow-listed edge-type variance.\n   - Bakeoff integration: report offender partition count and trend over cycles.\n\n4. TEST COVERAGE. The tool itself needs unit tests (synthetic behavior-map fixtures with known offender / non-offender partitions; assert tool reports them correctly).\n\nPRACTICAL NOTE: this test is GENUINELY USEFUL DURING Phase 2/3 (sibling items WI-sahab-fatoz-zosaj-nopuk-nozon-dosud-mogal-juhim and WI-mokam-jalig-fuhab-gifug-sigij-gotar-sisig-zufoz). Each migration shrinks the offender set; the report acts as a progress meter for the producer-side cleanup. Building this test BEFORE OR ALONGSIDE Phase 3 is high-leverage — it both validates the migration and surfaces remaining producer-side work that the static lint cannot see (because static only checks consumer-side sets, not actual emit sites).\n\nDEPENDENCIES (all SOFT):\n- ADR-0023 advancement to Proposed/Accepted. The partition-key choice is part of the ADR decision; if the ADR is amended, the test needs to track.\n- Bakeoff infrastructure availability. The standalone-CLI variant has no bakeoff dependency; the bakeoff-integration follow-on does.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- Standalone CLI script scripts/check-edge-type-runtime-coherence accepts a behavior-map JSON path, prints offender partitions with their edge_type variance, exits 0 if clean (or all offenders are allow-listed) and 1 otherwise.\n- Initial allow-list seeded from a current production run; documented in this item's discussion thread before merge.\n- Unit tests covering synthetic offender / non-offender fixtures, including the allow-list bypass path.\n- (Optional / follow-on tracker item, not in scope here) bakeoff integration: a BROAD or DEEP bakeoff cycle's reflect step runs the tool and surfaces the offender count.\n\nSIBLINGS:\n- WI-sahab-fatoz, WI-mokam-jalig, WI-vomoj-suhaz — ADR-0023 §6 Phase 2/3/4. The runtime test is INSTRUMENTAL to Phase 2/3 — building it before/during those phases is high-leverage. Phase 4 expectation is the offender set goes empty (modulo allow-list).\n- WI-variv-lujug-fobig-nojit-piraz-lojam-lizil-dosip — drift-linter-tightening. Static enforcement pair to this runtime enforcement; both should converge to 'axis-canonical-only' after Phase 4b.\n- WI-tavas-voror-rakiv-litog-gatad-zonoz-holah-tugil — 45-value triage. The runtime test surfaces partition-level offenders; WI-tavas-voror surfaces emit-site offenders. Different angles on the same producer-side reality; coordinate so neither rediscovers the other's findings.\n\nBACKGROUND: surfaced as loose-end #16 in the audit-lifecycle session (Apr 2026). ADR-0023 §3 specifies the runtime test alongside the static lint; only the static was built. Phase 1 of the migration plan was meant to land both 'initially configured to print a warning rather than fail' — only half landed."  # ff2d
    fields: {}  # ff2d
